  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 255 PM EDT Tue Sep 22 2026\n\n\n- Showers possible through far southern Michigan tomorrow \n\n Michigan currently is at an impasse between two systems. A low,\ncycling along the Iowa, Illinois and Wisconsin borders is \nbringing southerly moisture northward into lower michigan. A large\nupper level high remains entrenched over Ontario and Quebec. \nMichigan lies in between these systems. The pressure gradient \nalong their boundary is creating gusty surface winds out of the \neast. Expect these winds to continue through the evening, \ndampening briefly overnight, before strengthening again tomorrow. \nThe upper level high pressure will shift eastward as a ridge of \nhigh pressure builds front the south. This will drive the \npreviously mentioned low to the south east. As it moves east there\nis a slight chance that some of the precip bands on the \nperiphery will infiltrate Michigan with a slight chance of \nbringing light rain to the southern tier or two of Michigan \ntomorrow afternoon. Latest models continue to shift the low \nsouthward, so these precip chances continue to dwindle. \n\n- Fair, dry with warming weather into next week\n\n The models are in fair agreement through the mid to long range. The\nridge building from the south should become a full fledged rex \nblock high late this week, into the weekend. The low will continue\nto advect eastward and given the latest trends is tracking to \nclose off as it begins its trek up the northeastern seaboard. The \nhigh pressure will simultaneous keep this low from retrograding, \nand stop an upper level wave from intruding into the Great Lakes \nregion. \n\n The below normal temperatures will slowly warm through the\nweekend, with above normal temperatures, peaking into the low 70s,\nare trending into next week.",
